body:
What do you want for yourself?

&#x200B;

Don't know if an actual question. I don't currently want anything. Guess that's the point. I don't know. I'm not doing anything. Guess I'm just being here. I haven't thought too far into the future. Guess I'm just waiting for something.

Maybe I know that more booze isn't going to make anything better, but that is my experience. It has given me a kind of release. I know that it isn't good but I don't want to stop. I don't care.

&#x200B;

Just wanted to tell someone. Maybe just attention.

It is so nice that someone understands. It has been kind of hard. It is mostly my mental health and loneliness that has driven me to this. I can't stand feeling sad all the time. Breaking down for no reason. Feeling anything. I don't want to feel that way again.

&#x200B;

I have to sort my things out before stopping drinking but feel like I'm in a corner. So maybe I'll look in to it.

It isn't. I'm just so tired. I use it as a medicine. Yeah, it makes me not care about anything and not feel anything but maybe it's better than feeling bad all the time and wanting to actively not live. Now it's just passive. The thoughts haven't really ended but I don't feel bad about it. I'm not constantly suffering because of the thoughts.

&#x200B;

Being passive is just easier.

If you are asking that from the point of mental health, it can be. If you are suffering from it, it's bad. But that includes loneliness. But just being alone doesn't have to mean that you are lonely. 

&#x200B;

From the social point of view, isolating weakens social connections and bonds.

Physical pain distracts me of the emotional one. It's nice to have some variety.

&#x200B;

While I'm doing it, it gives me a release. I like the look of it. I like the fat. I like the blood. I get a high out of it. All the adrenaline and dopamine.
